    "encrypted": "false",
    "long_instructions": "## Do This:\r\n\r\n- Give your code and your backpack sketch to the AI Tutor, and ask it to build a Theater scene that matches what you drew.\r\n- Open your backpack and import the sketch you saved in SketchLab.\r\n- Using `input()`, create a **string** variable for each label on your sketch that calls for text (for example: a name, a color, a mascot, whatever your sketch labeled).\r\n- Create one **int** variable and one **bool** variable, assigned directly (not with `input()`).4\r\n- Reassign one of your variables based on a new value, for example, updating a score after another point is scored.\r\n- Write `print()` statements to show all of your variables, including the one you reassigned.\r\n- Before you run the AI's code, **predict** what you think it will draw.\r\n- Run it. Compare the result to your prediction and your sketch, did it match?\r\n- Write a short note: what did you keep, change, or reject from the AI's code, and why?\r\n\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n::::details [Why can't I use input() for my int and bool variables?]\r\n`input()` always gives you back text, even if you type a number like `5`, Python stores it as the string `\"5\"`, not the number `5`. Turning text into a number or a true/false value is something we haven't covered yet, so if you try to do math with a variable from `input()`, you'll hit an error you don't have the tools to fix. That's why these two get assigned directly instead.\r\n::::\r\n\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n<i class=\"fa-solid fa-laptop\"></i> **Coding Tip**\r\n\r\nStart small, then build! Test your input and print statements first, before you add the AI-generated part. If you use a color for one of your variables, stick to common color names (like `\"gold\"` or `\"crimson\"`), made-up or two-word colors may not render.",
